--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Thursday, the 8th of January, 1931 (3/3 key facts kept) ---
To-day I had a letter from my sister Lizzie, saying that the hop-picking will begin in September, and Bert may have work for three weeks in the gardens at Paddock Wood. I was glad to hear it, for he has been out of employment now for more than a month; but I thought of the risk to his health in sleeping in the fields, and wished the harvest had begun earlier. I read in the evening paper that there was mutiny among the sailors at Devonport, and was sorry to learn it. I also read of Marshal Joffre's funeral in Paris, and was grieved to think that he should be dead. Mrs. Mahoney asked me for the loan of two shillings, but I could not spare them.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Thursday, the 8th of January, 1931 (0/3 key facts kept) ---
1. Read in the Daily Herald that Britons were astonished at reports of insubordination at Devonport.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Thursday, the 8th of January, 1931 (1/1 key facts kept) ---
1. Read in _The Times_ that the auditors come to the office on Monday.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that she saw Lizzie walking with a strange man in a field.
   ☾ Arthur: He dreamt that the auditors came, and he was terribly nervous.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he died, and went straight to heaven.

